# Demo server - tools/server documentation. Sources: UI.md, EDITOR.md, ANNOTATIONS.md,
# YAMLOVER_EDITOR.yo, QUERY_EDITOR.yo - progress in ../../DOCSMIGRATION.md.
Demo server
description: the yamlover web server - the UI, the projectional editors and their state machines
- >
  `tools/server` (`npx yamlover <root>`) serves a web app that browses a yamlover project as a
  graph of nodes and edits it **in place**. This chapter is its documentation: [operating the
  UI](*::server:ui) is the user-facing guide, [the projectional editor](*::server:editor) is the
  maintainer's architecture map, and [annotations](*::annotations) covers fragments and
  tagging. The editors' behavior is specified state by state in three machine chapters - [the
  yamlover editor](*::server:yamlover-editor) (the source projection's typing grammar), [the
  chapter editor](*::server:chapter-editor) (the prose projection), and [the query
  editor](*::server:query-editor) (the breadcrumb, pointer cells, and tag picker) - each state a
  subchapter, its transitions a table of links.
- >
  This documentation is dogfood: the book you are reading is served by the very server it
  describes, and these machine chapters were edited with the editors they specify.
- >
  **What a crawler sees.** The shell is one static file served at every route, so the server fills
  its `<head>` per request: the node's title and description, a canonical URL with the query string
  dropped (so `?format=`, `?depth=` and the rest collapse onto one address), and Open Graph tags.
  `GET /sitemap.xml` lists the book's pages — the chapter spine, not every addressable node, since
  a scalar three levels inside a table is part of a page rather than one of them. `GET /robots.txt`
  answers too, though only a tree served at the origin root has a robots.txt anyone reads: under a
  base path the file that governs is the host's own. An address that resolves to nothing still
  answers 200 — the shell has to, since some working pages have no node behind them at all — and
  the client marks that page `noindex` when it finds nothing there.
